{"results":[{"url":"https://www.bea.gov/data/gdp/gross-domestic-product","final_url":"https://www.bea.gov/data/gdp/gross-domestic-product","title":"Gross Domestic Product | U.S. Bureau of Economic Analysis (BEA)","description":null,"language":"en","text":"# Gross Domestic Product\n\n## GDP (Advance Estimate), 3rd Quarter 2026\n\n|  |  |\n| --- | --- |\n| Q3 2026 (Adv) | +3.4% |\n| Q2 2026 | +2.2% |\n\nReal gross domestic product (GDP) increased at an annual rate of 3.4 percent in the third quarter of 2026 (July, August, and September), according to the advance estimate released today by the U.S. Bureau of Economic Analysis (BEA). In the second quarter, real GDP increased 2.2 percent. The contributors to the increase in real GDP in the third quarter were increases in investment, consumer spending, and government spending that were partly offset by a decrease in exports. Imports, which are a subtraction in the calculation of GDP, increased.\n\nCurrent Release\n\n* **Current release:** October 29, 2026\n* **Next release:** November 25, 2026\n\n#### Real GDP, Percent Change from Preceding Quarter, Q3 '26 (Adv) HPF\n\n#### Real GDP, Percent Change from Preceding Quarter, Q3 '26 (Adv) HPF\n\n### Current Release\n\n#### GDP (Advance Estimate), 3rd Quarter 2026\n\n- Full Release\n\n  PDF\n- Historical Comparisons\n\n  XLSX\n- Vintage History\n\n  XLSX\n- Key source data and assumptions\n\n  XLSX\n- Revision Information\n- Additional Information\n\n* Current-Dollar GDP: Years, Since 1929\n* Current-Dollar GDP: Quarters, Since 1947\n* Underlying Detail Tables\n\n  \\*\\_GA\\_J4698JNNFT\\*MTCYODQWNDK1MC4XNTAUMS4XNZI4NDA2MDU2LJQ3LJAUMA\n* Motor vehicles\n\n  XLSX\n  November 4, 2026\n* Additional Tables\n\n### Information on Updates\n\n* Latest Update\n* Information on previous updates\n\n* Data Archive\n\n  HTML?HMI=2\n  Previously published estimates contain historical data and have since been revised.\n* News Release Archive\n\n### What is Gross Domestic Product?\n\nA comprehensive measure of U.S. economic activity. GDP measures the value of the final goods and services produced in the United States (without double counting the intermediate goods and services used up to produce them). Changes in GDP are the most popular indicator of the nation's overall economic health.\n\nLearn More\n\n### Contact Personnel\n\n* GDP\n\n  Lisa Mataloni\n\n  301-278-9083\n\n  gdpniwd@bea.gov\n\n* Media\n\n  Connie O’Connell\n\n  301-278-9003\n\n  Connie.OConnell@bea.gov","author":null,"published_date":null,"latency_ms":1012.11702899991,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}